proof.text:
A bijection natural in the target. Fix an $R$-module $M$ and put $A=g^*\mathcal F_M$, $B=\mathcal F_{S\otimes_RM}$. For every $\mathcal O_Y$-module $\mathcal H$ compose the four bijections
(a) $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O_Y}(A,\mathcal H)\to\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O_X}(\mathcal F_M,g_*\mathcal H)$, $\psi\mapsto g_*\psi\circ\eta_{\mathcal F_M}$, the adjunction $g^*\dashv g_*$ [def-pc-module-pullback];
(b) $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O_X}(\mathcal F_M,g_*\mathcal H)\to\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,\Gamma(X,g_*\mathcal H))=\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H))$, $\varphi\mapsto\varphi_X\circ c_M$ [lem-weil-pbc-associated-module-hom] on $X$, with Step 1;
(c) $\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H))\to\operatorname{Hom}_S(S\otimes_RM,\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H))$, the inverse of $\chi\mapsto(m\mapsto\chi(1\otimes m))$ [lem-weil-pbc-scalar-extension-hom], for the $S$-module $N=\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H)$, whose restriction to $R$ is the module of (b) by Step 1;
(d) $\operatorname{Hom}_S(S\otimes_RM,\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H))\to\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O_Y}(B,\mathcal H)$, the inverse of $\chi\mapsto\chi_Y\circ c_{S\otimes_RM}$ [lem-weil-pbc-associated-module-hom] on $Y$ (whose ring of global sections is $S$).
Call the composite $\Phi_{\mathcal H}$. Each of (a)-(d) is natural in $\mathcal H$ (for (b) and (c) because $(g_*\beta)_X=\beta_Y$ by Step 1, and the naturality clauses of the cited nodes), so $\Phi_{\mathcal H'}(\beta\circ\psi)=\beta\circ\Phi_{\mathcal H}(\psi)$ for $\mathcal O_Y$-linear $\beta:\mathcal H\to\mathcal H'$. Tracing an element: (a) followed by (b) sends $\psi$ to $m\mapsto(g_*\psi)_X(\eta_{\mathcal F_M,X}(c_M(m)))=\psi_Y(g^*c_M(m))$; hence $\chi=\Phi_{\mathcal H}(\psi)$ is the unique $\mathcal O_Y$-linear map $B\to\mathcal H$ with $\chi_Y(c_{S\otimes_RM}(1\otimes m))=\psi_Y(g^*c_M(m))$ for all $m\in M$. In particular the map $\psi\mapsto(m\mapsto\psi_Y(g^*c_M(m)))$, $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O_Y}(A,\mathcal H)\to\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,\Gamma(Y,\mathcal H))$, being (b)$\circ$(a), is injective.
